From c7e36bff6d93fd383aba8770d95282be06669233 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:39:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] refactor(wire): the request-kind table read by schemas
 and constants; rollRequestSchema's rules by group

R1.4.4 (W-a's part): rollRequestSchema reads ROLL_KIND_INFO in place of its own lists: the
activity checks (`check`), FROM_TEXT (`fromText`), LINKABLE and the keyed kinds (`keyed`),
ANSWERS (`answers`), the damaging kinds (`damage`) and the four dice branches (`dice`); its four
Sets go. EFFECT_DICE_KINDS and COMPANION_UNTAKEN stay literals in constants/sending-stone.ts:
deriving them there would make constants/sending-stone.ts and constants/roll-kinds.ts import
each other (roll-kinds reads ROLL_KINDS at load), and C9 pins COMPANION_UNTAKEN's own order; the
table's equality test already holds both to it.

R1.4.7: the request's superRefine (cognitive complexity 84) runs ordered rule groups (typed dice,
naming, hit dice, targets, casting, damage, links, keys, enchantment, own fields), then the dice
rule its kind's `dice` names; the transfer's (17) runs its share and move rules. `issuesTo()`
stands for the 44 addIssue copies and `inFaces()` for the four results-in-faces checks. The
schema is annotated `z.ZodType<RollRequestInput>` in place of `as unknown as`; for tsc to accept
it, the damage modifiers' size refine is written as the type guard it is (`faces is DieSize`,
the same expression), as the hand type DamageModifiers already says. Every message and path,
and the issue order, are as before: a seeded comparison of the old and new schemas over 200,000
requests (196,215 reaching the rules, every issue path among them) found no difference.

Checks: an L1 per item (R/tools/l1.sh on a snapshot of the worktree), each green: jest 306
suites, 9,700 tests, 0 red; typegen and tsc clean; eslint 0 errors, 659 warnings (the two
complexity warnings in schemas/roll-request.ts gone); gates 1-12 ok (cycle groups 3, base 4).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
